README-CIO -- running the starred programs==========================================92 of the programs on this disk are marked with a star in DOC/INDEX.  Theyneed Microware's `cio` trap handler (a few also want `csl` and `math`).Without it they stop immediately with    **** Can't install trap handler ****They are not broken.  `cc -qixm` -- C library in a shared trap handler ratherthan linked into each binary -- was the normal way to build for OS-9, and itis why these are a fraction of the size of the trap-free ones.  What ismissing is a licensed Microware module, and if you run OS-9 you have it.`cio`, `csl` and `math` ship with Microware's OS-9 SDK.  They are not part ofthis collection, so this file explains how to point the disk at yours.(DOC/README-RUNNING covers the same three arrangements in more detail,including the /h0 paths.  This file is about cio specifically.)------------------------------------------------------1. You have an OS-9 system disk of your own------------------------------------------------------The usual case, and the easiest.  Point os9exec at your own disk and mountthis one alongside, so that /dd stays yours -- your CMDS, your LIB, your cio -- andthis collection is simply another drive:    OS9DISK=/path/to/your/os9disk  OS9H1=/path/to/freeware/dd  os9exec shellThen put this disk's commands on your execution path:    chx /h1/CMDSEverything runs, starred or not, because cio comes from your own /dd/CMDS.Add /h1/CMDS to your startup file if you want it permanently.------------------------------------------------------2. You want THIS disk as /dd, and you have cio------------------------------------------------------Copy the three modules onto this disk and everything works:    copy /h1/CMDS/cio  /dd/CMDS/cio    copy /h1/CMDS/csl  /dd/CMDS/csl    copy /h1/CMDS/math /dd/CMDS/math(where /h1 is your own system disk).  `math` is only needed by the programsthat do floating point -- see DOC/README-FLOATINGPOINT, which also explainswhy you may want math881 instead.The disk you end up with then contains modules from your own SDK, so keepthat copy for your own use -- the collection as published stays as it is.------------------------------------------------------3. You have no OS-9 system disk at all------------------------------------------------------Run with this disk as the only one:    OS9DISK=/path/to/freeware/dd os9exec bash_newThe 349 unstarred programs all run -- that is the whole point of the disk,and it is why so much of it was rebuilt trap-free.  The 92 starred ones willnot, and cannot without a licensed cio.Where a starred program mattered, there is usually a trap-free alternativealready here:    starred           use instead    -------           -----------    *ed  *emacs       vi, sedt, sed    *vi_cio           vi (the EFFO build)    *ksh              bash, sh, gshell    *zoo              lha, tar, gzip    *fgrep            grep    *ckermit          kermit    *cat.cio          cat    *rcs and friends  no free replacement on this diskDOC/INDEX marks every one of them, so you can tell before you try.